---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { NAP_MINUTES, courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { deckNote } from "../lib/deck";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const deck = await deckNote();

// `ContentLayout` already renders `description` as the standfirst under the
// hero, so nothing below restates it — the long-form course description is
// distributed across the headings instead of printed twice at two lengths.
---

<SiteLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A hypnogram drawn as a two-ink print: a black stepped line descends from awake through light sleep to the deep stage, then climbs back through REM over ninety minutes. A gold band shades the first twenty minutes, and a small circle marks where the line still sits in light sleep when that band ends."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    The nap is a biological need that modern work schedules erased. This course
    studies its science, its history, and its return. Every week comes back to
    that line, and by the end you are expected to have an argument about it that
    survives contact with someone who runs an institution.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Nap on purpose, keep records, and defend a policy — in that order, because
    each one needs the last. You will measure your own alertness with two
    deliberately unglamorous instruments, nap under observation in the {
      sessionLabels.plural
    }, and keep a fortnight's log of what your own sleep actually does. Then you
    will read how the nap was removed from the working day, how other societies
    kept it, and who is permitted to be seen sleeping now.
  </p>
  <p>
    You leave with a nap policy for a real institution — costed, argued for, and
    already survived one room of people whose job was to object to it.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape of the semester</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve weeks in three movements. <strong>Weeks 1–4 are the science</strong>:
    what a nap is, why length decides everything, and why the afternoon dip is
    on the clock rather than on the menu. <strong
      >Weeks 5–9 are the argument</strong
    >: industrial timekeeping pushed daytime sleep out of ordinary life, other
    cultures kept or lost it on their own terms, and permission to rest turns
    out to track social position more closely than it tracks need.
    <strong>Weeks 10–12 put it back</strong>: what the measuring devices can and
    cannot see, what sleep does for memory, and how to design a workplace,
    school or hospital that lets people sleep when their bodies ask to.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone, in any degree. This is a 1000-level breadth course and no prior
    knowledge of sleep science is assumed — the biology starts in the first
    lecture and never goes deeper than a first-year course can carry.
  </p>
  <p>
    You should expect to nap on purpose in front of other people, to record and
    hand in information about your own sleep, and to write a policy you can
    defend out loud. If any of those is a problem, read <a
      href={withBase("/policies/")}>the policies</a
    > before you enrol rather than after: each one has an alternative, and every
    alternative is easier to arrange in the first week than in the sixth.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        The weekly schedule. Each {sessionLabels.singular} carries that week's
        substance — what to bring, what happens in the room, what you leave with.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>
        Short, and each one ends with a claim you are asked to argue against.
        {deck && ` ${deck}`}
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>
        Three pieces, assembled across the teaching weeks rather than set at the
        end of them.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>
        Who teaches this, how they ended up on it, and what each of them is
        worth asking.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <h2>One more thing</h2>
  <p>
    This site has a <strong>Nap Mode</strong>. It dims the page, enlarges the
    reading text, folds the navigation away, and puts itself back to normal
    after {NAP_MINUTES} minutes — the nap length week 2 argues is the right one.
    The button sits at the bottom of the screen and stays there, because it is
    the switch that ends a nap early and should never be inside the menu it
    just closed.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
